# 📘 Security+ – Chapter 7
# Identity and Access Management (IAM)

---

## 1️⃣ Identity and Access Management (IAM)

Identity and Access Management (IAM) is a framework of policies and technologies that ensures the right individuals or entities have appropriate access to organizational resources.

### 🔹 Core Concepts

| Term | Definition |
|------|------------|
| Subject | A user or software requesting access |
| Object | A resource such as network, server, database, or file |
| Identification | Associating a valid subject with an account |
| Authentication | Verifying the identity of the subject |
| Authorization | Assigning rights, permissions, or privileges |
| Accounting (Auditing) | Tracking and logging user activity |

---

## 2️⃣ Authentication Design

Authentication design refers to creating secure mechanisms to verify user identity before granting access to resources.

---

## 🔐 Multifactor Authentication (MFA)

Multifactor Authentication (MFA) requires two or more verification factors.

### 🔹 Authentication Factors

| Factor Type | Example |
|-------------|----------|
| Something you know | Password, PIN |
| Something you have | Smart card, smartphone, hardware token |
| Something you are | Biometrics |
| Somewhere you are | Geolocation, IP address |
| Something you do | Behavioral biometrics (typing pattern) |

---

### 🔹 How MFA Works

1. User initiates login  
2. First factor verification (username/password)  
3. Second factor verification (SMS code, OTP, hardware token)  
4. Access granted or denied  

