## 7️⃣ Smart Card Authentication

Smart card authentication uses a physical card embedded with a microchip to authenticate users securely.

✔ Often used in enterprise environments  
✔ Supports certificate-based authentication  

---

## 8️⃣ RADIUS

Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) provides centralized AAA (Authentication, Authorization, Accounting).

✔ Commonly used for VPN access  
✔ Uses UDP (Port 1812 / 1813)

### 🔹 Components

- RADIUS Client  
- RADIUS Server  
- User Database  

---

## 9️⃣ TACACS+

TACACS+ is a Cisco-developed protocol used for centralized AAA.

✔ Uses TCP (Port 49)  
✔ Encrypts the entire packet  
✔ Separates Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting  

### 🔹 Components

- TACACS+ Client  
- TACACS+ Server  
- User Database  

---

## 🔟 OATH (Open Authentication)

OATH is an open standard initiative for authentication.

Supports:
- TOTP (Time-based One-Time Password)  
- HOTP (HMAC-based One-Time Password)  

Used in authentication applications such as:
- Google Authenticator  
- Microsoft Authenticator  

---

## 1️⃣1️⃣ Biometric Authentication

Biometric authentication verifies identity using physical or behavioral characteristics.

### 🔹 Examples

- Fingerprint  
- Retina scan  
- Facial recognition  
- Voice recognition  
- Behavioral typing pattern  

### 🔹 Advantages & Disadvantages

✔ High security  
✔ Non-transferable  
❌ Privacy concerns  
❌ Expensive implementation  

---

## 🎯 Interview Notes

- Kerberos is ticket-based authentication  
- RADIUS uses UDP  
- TACACS+ uses TCP  
- MFA significantly reduces credential-based attacks  
- Password managers reduce password reuse risk  

